This repository vendors the source of Speakeasy:

    Project:  Speakeasy
    Author:   Mandiant (Google Cloud)
    Source:   https://github.com/mandiant/speakeasy
    Commit:   1e6e622bb5cea2e4d843143fa15acf1043150291
    License:  MIT (see speakeasy_service/vendor/speakeasy-src/LICENSE.txt, copied verbatim)

speakeasy_service/vendor/speakeasy-src is a copy of the upstream `speakeasy` package plus
the packaging files needed to `pip install` it (pyproject.toml, setup.py, MANIFEST.in),
with six local modifications (described below). It is built and installed into this service's Docker image (see
Dockerfile) and invoked as a subprocess via its own `speakeasy` CLI entry point.

Local modification -- emulated clipboard. Upstream implements only GetClipboardSequenceNumber
(a constant), so a sample that reads the clipboard gets no text and faults before its own
logic runs. Changed files: speakeasy/winenv/api/usermode/user32.py (OpenClipboard,
CloseClipboard, EmptyClipboard, GetClipboardData, SetClipboardData, AddClipboardFormatListener,
RemoveClipboardFormatListener, IsClipboardFormatAvailable, and a GetClipboardSequenceNumber that
changes on every call), speakeasy/profiler_events.py (ClipboardEvent), speakeasy/profiler.py
(record_clipboard_event) and speakeasy/winenv/api/api.py (record_clipboard_event). The emulated
clipboard hands the sample one synthetic, wallet-address-shaped string per read (patterned
text with invalid checksums, never real addresses) and records what the sample writes back.

Local modification -- coalesced section protections. Upstream's PE loader
(speakeasy/windows/winemu.py, load_image) calls mem_protect once per 4 KiB page. Each call splits
a Unicorn memory region, so a PE with a very large section (a 32 MiB .data in one sample) spent
longer loading than the whole emulation timeout and never started running. Adjacent pages with
equal permissions are now protected in a single call (coalesce_page_perms); the resulting
permissions are identical.

Local modification -- CNG symmetric crypto. Upstream leaves BCryptSetProperty, BCryptGenerateSymmetricKey,
BCryptEncrypt and BCryptDecrypt unimplemented (a generic stub that reports success but writes no key handle
and no data), so a crypter that decrypts its payload with them never gets the plaintext. Changed files:
speakeasy/winenv/api/usermode/bcrypt.py (AES with CBC, ECB, CFB and GCM through pycryptodome, keys and IVs
tracked per handle; anything else returns STATUS_NOT_SUPPORTED), speakeasy/profiler_events.py (CryptoEvent),
speakeasy/profiler.py and speakeasy/winenv/api/api.py (record_crypto_event). The plaintext is stored as a
report artifact so the service can extract it.

Local modification -- version.dll. Added speakeasy/winenv/api/usermode/version.py (every query reports no
version information) and a "version" entry in speakeasy/config.py's default user modules, because a
reflective loader that cannot load one of its payload's imports aborts. Modules stay absent by default
(modules_always_exist is unchanged), so probes for sandbox DLLs still fail as they should.

Local modification -- optional real internet access. A new network.allow_internet config field
(default false) gates real TCP connects/sends/receives (winsock, speakeasy/winenv/api/usermode/
ws2_32.py) and real HTTP requests (WinInet, speakeasy/winenv/api/usermode/wininet.py) to PUBLIC
addresses through speakeasy/winenv/livenet.py; loopback, RFC 1918, link-local, multicast and cloud
metadata addresses are always refused regardless of this setting. DNS stays simulated either way.
Off by default; this service turns it on only when a submission sets allow_internet=true.

Local modification -- unmapped reads no longer end the run. windows/winemu.py's
_handle_invalid_read already mapped the faulting page zero-filled (as invalid writes do); it now
also lets emulation continue afterwards instead of stopping, so a sample that briefly reads a
stale or wrong pointer (e.g. a bad self-locate/header scan) can still reach the rest of its logic.
Each occurrence is still recorded on the run, and a run that keeps faulting like this in a loop
(over 1000 recovered reads) still ends, so this cannot spin forever.

The upstream git submodules deps/win32json (https://github.com/marlersoft/win32json,
commit 071df4995290b72320010ecfc2fca51946b5c6b4) and deps/phnt
(https://github.com/winsiderss/phnt, commit 53fbbdc5b5d2b08761db1c7b26bfa8c820924356)
were NOT vendored as source -- instead, they were checked out once locally and used
to run upstream's own `setup.py` signature-generation step (see
speakeasy-src/scripts/gen_win32_signatures.py and gen_phnt_signatures.py), and only
the resulting generated artifacts are vendored here:
speakeasy_service/vendor/speakeasy-src/speakeasy/resources/win32/{signatures,phnt_signatures}.json.gz.
This is exactly what upstream's own build does when those submodules ARE present at
build time (setup.py: "regenerate the generated speakeasy/resources/win32/*.json.gz
files... so that [they] ship in every distribution without being committed to the
repository") -- we just ran that generation step once ourselves and committed the
result, rather than requiring deps/win32json and deps/phnt to be fetched on every
Docker build. Without these files (or the submodules) present, `pip install .` hard
fails at build time with "deps/win32json is not checked out and no signature database
exists" -- this is not actually optional, unlike what upstream's own
`git submodule update --init ... || true` in its Dockerfile might suggest.
win32json is MIT licensed; phnt is MIT licensed (see each project's own repository).

